The ACPI resource descriptors `EFI_PCI_ROOT_BRIDGE_IO_PROTOCOL::Configuration`
answered with on the one root bridge of the ThinkPad T14 this repository is
tested on, off that machine's own firmware.

  root-bridge-0.bin   186 bytes, off the `Root bridge: 0 (segment 0) 186 bytes:`
                      line in the `loader.log` the stick came back with from
                      metal run 33 (`tests/metalcase`).

As with `../ovmf-pure-efi/SOURCE`: the bootloader printed the bytes and nothing
decoded them on the way, and what says the length was right is the file itself —
four descriptors whose own declared lengths tile it exactly, then the End Tag.
Each of the four also carries two accounts of its own extent, and all four agree
(`min + len - 1 == max`).

**This firmware answers the hull it has allocated, not the aperture the platform
gave it.** The same machine's `_CRS`, as Linux reports it (`journalctl -k -b 0`,
`pci_bus 0000:00: root bus resource ...`):

  window        _CRS                         Configuration()
  memory, low   0xa0800000-0xbfffffff        0xa2000000-0xbcffffff
  memory, high  0x4000000000-0x7fffffffff    0x4000000000-0x603dbfffff
  I/O           0x0000-0x0cf7, 0x0d00-0xffff 0x3000-0x3fff
  bus           00-79                        00-79

The protocol's contract is the root bridge's *current* settings (UEFI 2.10
§14.2), so an answer is always inside the platform's aperture and may be far
inside it. That is the direction that is safe: a BAR placed inside this range is
inside a range the bridge decodes, on a machine where nothing else says so.
